# gtools/encode.golfj — `tools/codepage.py encode`, written in GOLF (M-TOOL).
#
# A stdin -> stdout filter: glyph / `\mnemonic` / ASCII source in, raw code-page
# bytes out.  This is the tool the build leans on hardest — every `.golfj` in
# the repo, `self/golf2.golfj` included, goes through the Python version of it
# on the way to a binary.
#
# It SHADOWS the Python; it does not replace it.  `tools/codepage.py` stays the
# build path and the source of truth, and this file is trusted exactly as far as
# `test/gtools.sh` shows it emitting BYTE-IDENTICAL output on every real source
# in the repo.  The bootstrap ladder never calls it, so the `golf2 == golf2'`
# fixpoint is exactly as trustworthy as it was before.
#
# Build and run:
#     gtools/build gtools/encode.golfj build/gencode
#     build/gencode < self/golf2.golfj > /tmp/golf2.gb
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ALGORITHM, which is `codepage.encode()` line for line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   1. `#`  a comment: skip to (not including) the next newline, emit nothing.
#           There is NO string awareness at all — a `#` inside a “…“ literal
#           really does eat the rest of the line.  That is not an oversight
#           being ported faithfully out of politeness: `self/golf2.golfj` says
#           in so many words that a comment may contain anything, and the blob
#           is spliced in AFTER encoding precisely because a `#` in it would be
#           swallowed.  Diverging here would change what the repo's sources mean.
#   2. `\`  an escape.  `\\` emits byte 0x5C (swap) and consumes two.  Otherwise
#           scan the maximal run of ASCII letters and take its LONGEST KNOWN
#           MNEMONIC PREFIX — `\sqr48` is `\sqr` then `48`, `\sqrp` is `\sqr`
#           then the word `p` — emit that byte and resume at the unconsumed
#           tail.  No known prefix is fatal.
#   3. a glyph in the code page -> its byte.  TESTED BEFORE THE ASCII
#           PASSTHROUGH, and it must stay that way: the five ASCII glyphs
#           (`~ $ | = >`) map to their own code points today, so the two orders
#           agree, but the Python consults `GLYPH2BYTE` first and a glyph that
#           ever stops being an identity would silently change meaning here.
#   4. any other byte < 128 -> itself (whitespace included).
#   5. anything else is fatal: not in the code page.
#
# Both fatal cases write one line to fd 2 and exit(1), matching Python's
# `raise SystemExit(...)`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THE TABLE COMES FROM
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `data/codepage.tsv`, loaded with `lib/tutf.golfj`'s `t`.  A GOLF program
# cannot carry the code page as source literals — a “…“ block cannot contain
# byte 0x8E, which is the block delimiter — so the table has to be a file.
#
#   * `argv[1]`, when the tool is given one: the path to the table.
#   * otherwise `data/codepage.tsv`, relative to the working directory.
#
# The default is what makes this a drop-in for the Python, which takes no path
# at all: `test/gtools.sh` chdirs to `expanded/` and its `diff_filter` helper
# runs every tool as a bare `tool < file > out` with no arguments, so a tool
# that REQUIRED a path would have meant changing a helper four sibling tools
# share.  A relative default is fragile on its own — run this from anywhere but
# `expanded/` and it cannot find the table — which is exactly why the argv
# override exists and why the failure says which file and how to fix it.
#
# That message is the one place this file does its own `⎈` rather than calling
# `lib/tio.golfj`'s `b`: `b` dies with "tio: cannot open file for reading",
# which names neither the file nor the override, and a wrong working directory
# is the single most likely way to hold this tool wrong.  Every other syscall
# here goes through the library.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FIDELITY: where the port could diverge, and why it does not
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * PYTHON WALKS CHARACTERS, THIS WALKS BYTES.  `codepage.py` decodes stdin as
#   UTF-8 first.  It does not matter: `#`, `\`, `\n` and every ASCII byte are
#   single-byte in UTF-8 and can never appear inside a multi-byte sequence, and
#   `v` matches whole glyphs (see below), so the two agree position for
#   position.  Input that is not valid UTF-8 is the one difference — Python
#   raises `UnicodeDecodeError`, this reports the offending byte — and no source
#   in this repo is such a file.
#
# * `isalpha()` IS UNICODE, `lib/ttext.golfj`'s `j` IS ASCII-ONLY.  Python would
#   read `\café` as the four-letter run "café"; `j` stops at the `é`.  Every
#   mnemonic in ../REGISTRY.md §1 is ASCII by the rule that they are `\word`
#   escapes typed on an ASCII keyboard, and the scan is only ever consulted
#   immediately after a `\`, so the two agree on every input that is not already
#   a fatal error.  `ttext`'s own header documents this; it is restated here
#   because THIS is the file where it would bite.
#
# * GLYPH MATCHING NEEDS NO UTF-8 DECODER.  No glyph's UTF-8 encoding is a
#   prefix of another's — UTF-8 guarantees it, a continuation byte can never
#   start a sequence — so at most one row can match at a given position and `v`
#   may work on the raw byte stream.  `v` takes the longest match anyway, so
#   correctness rests on the algorithm and not on that fact.
#
# * THE MESSAGES.  "codepage: unknown mnemonic \name" is Python's text exactly,
#   and it is built by writing the backslash and the alpha run STRAIGHT OUT OF
#   THE INPUT BUFFER — which is why no 0x5C appears in this source (the house
#   rule the prelude and all three libraries keep: `decode` renders 0x5C as a
#   bare `\`, which `encode` would then read as an escape).  The other message
#   is the deliberate divergence: Python says `char 'é' (U+00E9) is not in the
#   code page`, which needs a UTF-8 decoder and Python's `repr` quoting rules to
#   reproduce, so this says `byte 233 is not in the code page` instead.  Stream
#   (fd 2) and exit status (1) match in both cases, which is what a caller can
#   actually test.
#
# * WHAT STDOUT HOLDS WHEN THE TOOL DIES.  Python builds the whole result in
#   memory and raises before writing a byte of it, so a failed run leaves an
#   empty file.  This streams through `g`'s 8192-byte buffer, so a failure in
#   the first 8 KB also leaves nothing (`a` deliberately does not flush) but one
#   after that leaves whatever had already been flushed.  That is `lib/tio.golfj`'s
#   stated error policy, not an accident of it — "half of a tool's output is
#   worse than none of it: the whole point of dying is that the artifact is not
#   written", and a partial file is never a complete one.  The exit status is
#   the contract either way, and no caller in this repo consumes the stdout of a
#   run that failed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NAMES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Words: `B` and `C`, the two free uppercase letters (../REGISTRY.md §2).  They
# are free for the PRELUDE to take one day; nothing in the prelude or the three
# tool libraries defines them today, and a gtools program is compiled on its
# own, so borrowing them here costs nothing and collides with nothing.  All 26
# lowercase letters are library words and none may be redefined.
#
# Variables (→x/←x — a namespace of its own, unrelated to word names, exactly as
# `self/golf2.golfj` uses `←T` next to the prelude word `T`).  Uppercase, so
# that `←J` reading a cell never has to be told apart from `j` calling `ttext`'s
# is-letter at a glance:
#
#   P  the input cursor            J  one past the alpha run after a `\`
#   N  one past the last input byte  K  bytes the current match consumed
#   V  the op byte that match found  U  a scratch address   Q  a scratch length
#   T  the default path's buffer (top level only)
#
# `s` is NEVER touched: it is `lib/tutf.golfj`'s state block and `←s` is how
# every one of its words finds the table.
#
# NOTHING WIDE AND NOTHING NEGATIVE IS PARKED IN A VARIABLE.  ../REGISTRY.md
# §2.1/§3 is the authority on how wide a bank cell is and whether `←x` sign- or
# zero-extends; this file simply does not depend on the answer.  Every value it
# stores is a small count or an address inside the heap or the code arena, and
# the two kinds that would care never go near a cell:
#
#   * the -1 `v`/`w` push for "no match" — they report it a second time as
#     `k == 0`, so this reads the COUNT and drops the marker into `V` unread,
#     which is the same reason it never needs a signed test;
#   * `argv[1]`, a stack pointer far above 2^32 — the one genuinely 64-bit value
#     this program handles, and it NEVER LEAVES THE DATA STACK (`1 h"[_←T]`).
#
# So the 4-byte hazard `ttext`'s header describes — `-1` returning as
# 4294967295 and a not-found branch silently not running — has no purchase
# here, and neither would a later widening of the bank.
#
# House rules, from the prelude's header: raw atoms (`\radd \rsub \rmin \rlt`)
# for every pointer, index and flag step, never the bare `+ - * ⌈ ⌊`, which
# M-VEC made polymorphic; `[…]` runs its body when TOS is 0 and `{…}` is a
# DO-while that loops while TOS is 0, so every loop whose trip count can be zero
# is guarded; flags are zero-is-true.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Words
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:B          # B : str ->   write a “…“ block to fd 2.  No newline, no exit — the
            # caller is mid-sentence and appends the rest itself.  (`a` is the
            # word that finishes one: block, newline, exit(1).)
→U          # park it: the block address is wanted twice
2 ←U4\radd ←U@ 1\sys_
            # write(2, str+4, str@) — a string is [len:4][bytes] and that length
            # prefix is still four bytes, so it is the 32-bit `@`.  Raw and
            # unchecked, like `a`'s own writes: this is the failure path.
;

:C          # C : ->   consume exactly ONE token at ←P, emitting its bytes and
            # advancing ←P past it.  A word, rather than the loop body inline,
            # so that each arm can `^` out instead of threading a "handled" flag
            # through three nested blocks.

# --- 1. `#` : a comment, dropped ------------------------------------------
←P?35\eq1\radd[                         # `≡` is -1 when equal, so `1﹢` makes it
                                        # the zero-is-true flag `[` wants
 {←P1\radd→P                            # step FIRST — buf[P] is the `#` itself,
                                        # which is not the newline being sought
  ←P←N\rlt1\radd                        # 1 once the cursor reaches the end ...
  ←P←N1\rsub\rmin?10\eq\or}             # ... or lands on a newline.  Reading
                                        # through `⊓` against N-1 is what keeps
                                        # the final step from touching buf[N].
 ^]                                     # the newline itself is left for the
                                        # next token, as Python's `continue`
                                        # leaves it: it encodes as whitespace

# --- 2. `\` : an escape ---------------------------------------------------
←P?92\eq1\radd[
 ←P1\radd←N\rlt1\radd[←P1\radd?92\eq1\radd[92 g ←P2\radd→P ^]]
                                        # `\\` -> one literal 0x5C, two consumed.
                                        # Nested rather than `$`-ed so buf[P+1]
                                        # is only read once it is known to exist.
 ←P1\radd→J                             # the maximal run of ASCII letters ...
 ←J←N\rlt1\radd[{←J?j"[←J1\radd→J]←J←N\rlt1\radd\rmax}]
                                        # `j` is 0 for a letter; the dup feeds
                                        # both the step and the stop flag, which
                                        # is "not a letter" OR "at the end"
 ←P1\radd←J w→K→V                       # ... and its longest KNOWN prefix.  `w`
                                        # takes the two ends of the run and does
                                        # the whole longest-match rule itself —
                                        # no loop at this call site at all.
 ←K[“codepage: unknown mnemonic “B      # K == 0: nothing matched.  Python's text
  2 ←P ←J←P\rsub 1\sys_                 # the `\` AND the run, straight out of
                                        # the input buffer — which is how this
                                        # source stays free of the byte 0x5C
  ““a]                                  # an empty block: `a` is here only for
                                        # its newline and its exit(1)
 ←V g ←P1\radd←K\radd→P ^]              # emit, and resume at the unconsumed tail

# --- 3. a code-page glyph, BEFORE the ASCII passthrough -------------------
←P←N v→K→V                              # `v` never reads past N, so this is safe
                                        # at the very last byte of the input
←K[                                     # K == 0: no glyph starts here

# --- 4. a plain ASCII byte, itself ----------------------------------------
 ←P?128\rlt1\radd[←P?g ←P1\radd→P ^]

# --- 5. anything else is fatal --------------------------------------------
 “codepage: byte “B
 1 A→U ←P?←U o→Q 2 ←U←Q 1\sys_          # `o` formats the byte as decimal into
                                        # eight bytes of heap, which is five
                                        # more than three digits can need
 “ is not in the code page“a]
←V g ←P←K\radd→P                        # a glyph matched: emit its byte and step
                                        # over ALL of its UTF-8 bytes
;


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Top level.  Prepended source runs before the program's own, so the prelude's
# allocator and tio's output buffer are already live by the time this starts.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The code-page table.  `b` wants a NUL-terminated path because open(2) does,
# and a “…“ literal is a counted block with arbitrary code after it — so the
# default path is copied into heap and a 0 appended before it can be opened.
“data/codepage.tsv“→U
3 A→T                                   # 24 bytes: 17 for the name, room to spare
←U4\radd ←T ←U@ m
0 ←T ←U@\radd,

# argv[1] if there is one, else that default.  `h` answers 0 past the end of
# argv, and the pointer it returns is a STACK address — far above 2^32 — so it
# stays on the data stack and is never parked in a variable.
1 h"[_←T]

# Opened here rather than through `b` so the diagnostic can name the file and
# the override; see the header.  The kernel returns a negative errno, not -1.
0 0 2\sys"0\slt1\radd[“encode: cannot open the code-page table (argv[1], or data/codepage.tsv relative to the working directory)“a]

# Slurp it and parse it.  The records point INTO this buffer, so it must — and
# does — stay alive for the whole run; the record count is of no use here.
e t _

# Slurp stdin.  `e` yields (buffer, length); N becomes one past the last byte,
# which is the form every loop below wants and `v`/`w` take as their window end.
0 e→N→P ←N←P\radd→N

# The token loop.  Guarded, because empty input must produce empty output and
# `{…}` would otherwise run its body once on a buffer with no bytes in it.
←P←N\rlt1\radd[{C ←P←N\rlt1\radd}]

# The final flush.  Nothing else can know the program is done — `g` only flushes
# when its 8192 bytes fill up, so without this the tail of the output is lost.
f
